   1: **Original Paper**
   2: 
   3: # A Machine Learning Depression Screener Developed in the Korea Community Health Survey and Externally Validated in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey Across the COVID-19 Period: Development and External Validation Study
   4: 
   5: Authors: [to be completed]
   6: 
   7: Corresponding author: [to be completed]
   8: 
   9: ---
  10: 
  11: ## Structured Abstract
  12: 
  13: **Background.** Depression is a leading cause of disability, and scalable, low-cost screening is needed. Most machine learning (ML) depression models from national surveys report internal validation only; whether they transport to another country and remain stable across the COVID-19 shift is rarely tested.
  14: 
  15: **Objective.** To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ￣10) in the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital triage tool.
  16: 
  17: **Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017?2025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES?14 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before analysis. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey?weighted with stratified cluster bootstrap CIs, plus SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021?2023; n=5,210) cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration.
  18: 
  19: **Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822?0.841), meeting the ￣0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735?0.808; post 0.769, 0.739?0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66?0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope⊥0.99, CITL⊥0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749⊥0.772, 汁+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753⊥0.769, 汁+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). A 7-item model, with predictor order and item count fixed entirely inside the development data, lost ¯0.013 AUROC everywhere (0.819 internal, 0.760 pre, 0.758 post; H4 supported). Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an in-domain oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765?0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81?0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
  20: 
  21: **Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
  22: 
  23: **Keywords:** depression; mass screening; machine learning; supervised machine learning; models, statistical; external validation; transportability; concept shift; COVID-19; mobile health; Patient Health Questionnaire; Republic of Korea.
  24: 
  25: ---
  26: 
  27: ## 1. Introduction
  28: 
  29: Depression is among the leading causes of years lived with disability worldwide [1], and its detection at the community and primary-care level remains incomplete. Brief self-report instruments such as the PHQ-9 enable population-scale screening [3], yet applying a fixed cut-off uniformly across heterogeneous populations does not prioritize those at highest risk. Risk-stratification tools that combine routinely available sociodemographic and behavioral information could support more efficient triage and, when delivered through web or mobile interfaces, could scale at low marginal cost?an application squarely within the scope of internet-based health services.
  30: 
  31: ML models for depression have been developed repeatedly from large national surveys, including the KCHS and the Korea National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey [11,12], typically reporting strong internal discrimination. However, most published models are assessed only through internal validation. Internal performance is an optimistic indicator of real-world behavior: it does not establish that a model transports to a different sampling frame, health system, or population, and it does not test stability under temporal distribution shift.
  32: 
  33: The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction [2,10]. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration?updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7]?restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.
  34: 
  35: This study addresses these gaps with a development-and-external-validation design well suited to the outcome. Depression is operationalized identically in both surveys (nine-item PHQ-9, 0?3 scoring, sum ￣10), minimizing outcome-harmonization error, while the surveys differ in country, health system, racial/ethnic composition, and survey mode, making NHANES a substantively external validation set. NHANES additionally provides NCHS-defined pre- and post-pandemic data, enabling temporal validation within the same external population.
  36: 
  37: We pursued four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in KCHS; (2) externally validate it in US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately pre/post-COVID-19 and quantify recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model for a low-burden digital tool. We hypothesized: **H1** the full model achieves internal AUROC ￣0.80; **H2** external discrimination remains clinically useful while calibration may deteriorate; **H3** recalibration substantially improves calibration; **H4** the parsimonious model incurs only a small performance loss.
  38: 
  39: ---
  40: 
  41: ## 2. Methods
  42: 
  43: ### 2.1 Study design and reporting standards
  44: Retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation; reported per TRIPOD+AI (2024) [4], risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST [5]. The study protocol and statistical analysis plan?including the four hypotheses?were written and dated before any analysis. The predictor-set expansion described in ＝2.5 is a documented amendment written *after* the 14-predictor model had already been developed and applied to NHANES, and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted (＝2.5.1). Multimedia Appendix 2 provides the year-specific harmonization crosswalk together with an English rendition of the locked amendment; the unmodified original protocol, statistical analysis plan, and amendment document will be deposited with the analysis code, hyperparameter grids, and model card in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]; see Data Availability).
  45: 
  46: ### 2.2 Data sources
  47: **Development ? KCHS.** Nationwide KDCA survey of community-dwelling adults, annual in-person household interviews (~230,000/year), stratified/clustered/weighted complex-sample design. Pooled development sample: KCHS 2017?2025 with PHQ-9 administered; n=2,048,510. From this pooled sample, an outcome-stratified random split created a **500,000-record training set** and a **100,000-record internal hold-out set** used for all internal performance estimates; the remaining 1,448,510 records were not used. The training-set size was fixed for computational tractability before any external evaluation, and a subsequent check confirmed that expanding it toward the full pooled sample did not improve?and slightly reduced?external discrimination (＝3.5).
  48: **External validation ? US NHANES.** NCHS survey combining household interview and mobile examination center assessment; depression via the DPQ (PHQ-9) module. Two periods used as separate validation sets: pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020, "P_" files; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023, "_L" files; n=5,210).
  49: 
  50: ### 2.3 Participants
  51: Adults ￣20 years with all nine PHQ-9 items answered. Pregnant respondents were retained in the main analysis. A prior physician diagnosis of depression is not recorded in the KCHS 2017?2025 questionnaires and therefore could not be used for exclusion or sensitivity analysis; this is noted as a limitation.
  52: 
  53: ### 2.4 Outcome
  54: Current depression = PHQ-9 total ￣10, computed identically in both surveys (nine items, 0?3). KCHS items recoded 1?4⊥0?3 to match NHANES. Sensitivity: ￣5 cut-off.
  55: 
  56: ### 2.5 Candidate predictors and harmonization
  57: Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors?age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes?plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ㏒ NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ㏒ OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ㏒ FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
  58: 
  59: **2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)?(iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature?food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ?1.4) [13], unemployment (?1.9?2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]?supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
  60: 
  61: **Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)?(iii)?notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (汁AUROC <0.001; ＝3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (＝3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.
  62: 
  63: **Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020?2021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (＝2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (＝2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
  64: 
  65: ### 2.6 Missing data
  66: All missing-data handling was performed by a preprocessing pipeline fitted in the development training set and applied unchanged to the internal hold-out and to each external set (no leakage). In the gradient-boosting primary model, binary predictors were passed through with missingness preserved and handled natively by the tree learner?so structurally missing binary items such as living-alone in NHANES pre are never imputed to a constant?while continuous predictors were median-imputed and categorical predictors mode-imputed within the same pipeline. For the linear comparator, continuous predictors were median-imputed, categorical predictors mode-imputed, and binary predictors mode-imputed with an added missing indicator. Household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health and self-rated oral health are ordinal scores and were modeled as numeric variables rather than as unordered categories, so they were median-imputed and standardized. Alternative strategies (missing indicators added for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors) were examined in sensitivity analysis (＝2.12). A silent-column-omission check was added after a data-integrity audit (see ＝2.13).
  67: 
  68: ### 2.7 Sample size
  69: Development is not power-limiting given KCHS size: the 500,000-record training set contains approximately 18,000 events (about 1,000 events per candidate predictor) and the 100,000-record hold-out approximately 3,600 events. NHANES event counts (728 pre, 661 post; weighted prevalence 8.4% and 12.2%) govern external calibration and decision-curve precision.
  70: 
  71: ### 2.8 Model development
  72: Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations?main effects only, versus main effects plus four pre-specified interaction terms (age▼self-rated health, self-rated health▼oral health, age▼employment, income▼employment)?evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The primary model was selected a priori by cross-validated AUROC and then by |calibration slope ? 1|; the ranking was decided on the first criterion, so the further pre-specified tie-break on parsimony was never invoked. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM was selected (best internal CV-AUROC 0.840 vs random forest 0.835 and elastic net 0.822) and refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
  73: 
  74: ### 2.9 Performance evaluation
  75: Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey?weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>?1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>?1) times the selection count (1000 replicates) [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
  76: 
  77: ### 2.10 External validation strategy
  78: Geographic: KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting. Temporal/COVID: evaluated separately in pre and post cycles. Recalibration: intercept/slope recalibration applied out-of-sample (cross-fit) and recovery quantified?updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7].
  79: 
  80: ### 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, deployment
  81: SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed in the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (＝3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
  82: 
  83: ### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
  84: Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (￣5 as well as ￣10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (⊥14), removal of food insecurity only (⊥16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and?as pre-specified in the protocol?a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total. The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (＝2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
  85: 
  86: ### 2.13 Data-integrity audit
  87: An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent ⊥ constant imputation) ? corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN ? corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile ? corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.
  88: 
  89: ### 2.14 Ethical Considerations
  90: KCHS and NHANES are public, de-identified secondary datasets. NHANES data collection was approved by the NCHS Research Ethics Review Board, and KCHS is conducted under the authority of the KDCA. The present secondary analysis was reviewed by the Institutional Review Board of Daejeon University, which granted an exemption from review (approval number [to be inserted]) on the grounds that the study uses publicly available and de-identified data, collects and records no personal identifiers, and poses no more than minimal risk (no participant contact, intervention, or biological sampling). No re-identification was attempted, no compensation was involved, and no individually identifiable information is reported.
  91: 
  92: ---
  93: 
  94: ## 3. Results
  95: 
  96: ### 3.1 Participant characteristics
  97: Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ￣10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates?e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively)?consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ￣20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential?higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents?so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ¯0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%⊥9.27% pre, 12.69%⊥12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
  98: 
  99: ### 3.2 Model development and internal performance
 100: LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (＝2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.822?0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 ? **H1 met**.
 101: 
 102: ### 3.3 Geographic and temporal (COVID) external validation
 103: See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.735?0.808)**, post **0.769 (0.739?0.799)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US?as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope⊥0.99, CITL⊥0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged ? **H2 and H3 supported**.
 104: 
 105: **Table 2. Discrimination and calibration (primary model, LightGBM, 17 predictors, weighted).**
 106: 
 107: | Metric | Internal | NHANES pre | pre (recal.) | NHANES post | post (recal.) |
 108: |---|---|---|---|---|---|
 109: | AUROC | 0.832 | 0.772 | 0.769 | 0.769 | 0.767 |
 110: | 95% CI | 0.822?0.841 | 0.735?0.808 | ? | 0.739?0.799 | ? |
 111: | AUPRC | 0.233 | 0.267 | 0.262 | 0.346 | 0.346 |
 112: | 95% CI | 0.215?0.251 | 0.211?0.332 | ? | 0.307?0.390 | ? |
 113: | Brier | 0.031 | 0.071 | 0.070 | 0.099 | 0.093 |
 114: | ECE | 0.004 | 0.032 | ? | 0.067 | ? |
 115: | Calibration slope | 1.00 | 0.66 | 0.99 | 0.68 | 0.99 |
 116: | CITL | +0.10 | +0.49 | 0.00 | +1.11 | 0.00 |
 117: 
 118: CIs are from a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates; ＝2.9). CITL is the offset-model calibration intercept, so positive values denote under-prediction of observed risk. Expected calibration error (ECE) confirms excellent internal calibration (0.004) with drift on external application (0.032 pre, 0.067 post), consistent with the calibration pattern and recoverable by recalibration.
 119: 
 120: Recalibration is intercept/slope updating applied out-of-sample (5-fold cross-fit); because each fold uses a separate monotone calibration map, the recalibrated AUROC may differ trivially (by ¯0.003) from the original and is not the target of recalibration (calibration slope/CITL are). Internal calibration uses the original probabilities. Race/ethnicity is used only for the NHANES fairness analysis, not as a predictor.
 121: 
 122: ### 3.4 Effect of the added predictors
 123: To avoid advantaging the larger predictor set through tuning, the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search under the identical internal protocol (＝2.8; best internal CV-AUROC 0.824). The three added predictors improved external discrimination over this independently tuned 14-predictor model: pre 0.749⊥**0.772** (汁+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; *P*<.001), post 0.753⊥**0.769** (汁+0.016, 95% CI +0.008 to +0.023; *P*<.001); *P* values are from a paired rescaled cluster bootstrap with replicates centred on the null and a Monte-Carlo correction (2000 replicates), with internal AUROC rising 0.822⊥0.832. Independent tuning did not help the 14-predictor model?running it with the 17-predictor configuration gave nearly identical results (pre 0.750, post 0.754)?so the gain reflects predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Removing food insecurity alone (16-predictor model) retained most of the gain (pre 0.767, post 0.760), confirming the improvement does not depend on the single most weakly harmonized predictor.
 124: 
 125: ### 3.5 Why external discrimination plateaus at ~0.77: concept shift
 126: Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments: feature engineering, hyperparameter search, heterogeneous ensembling, transfer-robust internal selection, domain adaptation, alternative losses, and structural variants) did not exceed external AUROC ~0.77 when model selection was restricted to KCHS-internal criteria. Apparent gains from externally-tuned variants (e.g., specific interaction terms, ensemble weights) did not survive leakage control.
 127: 
 128: We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate ＝2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765?0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.757?0.787 by stratified cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is also estimated on the pooled pre+post sample, whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, so the comparison is approximate. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81?0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise?and slightly lowered?external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (?0.003 pre, ?0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor?outcome relationship itself**?concept shift?although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (＝2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (＝3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
 129: 
 130: ### 3.6 Parsimonious deployment model
 131: Predictor order and item count were both fixed inside the training set (＝2.11). On the 50,000-record selection set, discrimination rose steeply to five items and then flattened: 1 item 0.753; 3 items 0.790; 5 items 0.820; 6 items 0.822; **7 items 0.829**; 8 items 0.832; 12 items 0.834; 17 items 0.835. Seven items was the smallest count within 0.01 AUROC of the full model on that selection set (0.829 vs 0.835; six items fell short at 0.822).
 132: 
 133: Evaluated afterwards on data never used for selection, the resulting 7-item model ? self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, self-rated oral health, employment, and partnership ? achieved hold-out AUROC **0.819**, NHANES pre **0.760** and NHANES post **0.758**, against 0.832/0.773/0.769 for the full 17-predictor model fitted on the same data. The loss is 0.013 internally, 0.012 pre-pandemic and 0.011 post-pandemic ? **H4 supported**, with the caveat that the protocol described the tolerable loss qualitatively rather than as a numeric margin. Adding smoking as an eighth item raised pre-pandemic discrimination to 0.770 but lowered post-pandemic discrimination to 0.753, so the seven-item set is preferred for deployment. The full k=1?17 trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
 134: 
 135: ### 3.7 Sensitivity analyses
 136: Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (＝3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ￣30 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20?39 (＝3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ￣5 lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea?US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ￣10 threshold to about 0.02 at ￣5 (0.759 vs 0.736?0.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. In the pre-specified continuous-score analysis, the deployed classifier ranked the untransformed PHQ-9 total with weighted Spearman 伏=0.378 internally, 0.403 pre-pandemic and 0.439 post-pandemic, and a model trained directly on the continuous score performed similarly (伏=0.402/0.403/0.424; weighted R昌 0.216/0.166/0.102), so the transportability pattern is not an artifact of dichotomizing the outcome. Data-integrity fixes (＝2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
 137: 
 138: ### 3.8 Interpretability, fairness, and clinical utility
 139: Global SHAP importance was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.58), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.30), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.24)**, partnership (0.17), and **employment (0.17)**; food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 11th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top seven, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman 伏=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor?outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 11th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
 140: 
 141: Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785?0.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811?0.833; male 0.831, 0.816?0.845), education (0.813?0.830), and age (20?39 years 0.785, 0.766?0.802 < 40?59 years 0.828, 0.812?0.843 < ￣60 years 0.859, 0.849?0.870). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated. Pre-pandemic: Mexican American 0.697 (0.625?0.777; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.727?0.800; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.673?0.871; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.733?0.818; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.727?0.825; n=2,798), non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.725?0.875; n=886). Post-pandemic: Mexican American 0.689 (0.609?0.794; n=330), non-Hispanic Black 0.727 (0.650?0.794; n=598), non-Hispanic White 0.770 (0.723?0.812; n=3,215), other/multiracial 0.786 (0.709?0.873; n=340), other Hispanic 0.790 (0.747?0.843; n=499), non-Hispanic Asian 0.841 (0.638?0.988; n=228). These intervals are wide and mutually overlapping, and all but the Mexican American interval overlap the cohort-level interval, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; that the lowest point estimate falls in Mexican American respondents in both cycles nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97?1.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93?1.03 including education strata; subgroup CITL ?0.02 to +0.26). In NHANES the subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.55?0.78, one exception) and subgroup CITL was positive in every group except Mexican American pre-pandemic (up to +1.30), confirming that the external miscalibration is under-prediction of absolute risk and not only over-extreme ranking (＝3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
 142: 
 143: On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110?0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948?0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)?a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4?and the 7-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.137 and 0.184; number needed to screen 7.3 and 5.4). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies?screening everyone and screening no one?at threshold probabilities of 0.10?0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4?S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
 144: 
 145: ---
 146: 
 147: ## 4. Discussion
 148: 
 149: ### 4.1 Principal Findings
 150: A depression-screening ML model developed in Korea and externally validated in the US met its internal target (AUROC 0.832) and retained clinically useful?though lower?discrimination (~0.77) on genuinely external US data and across the COVID-19 shift, with calibration recoverable by lightweight recalibration. Expanding the transportable predictor set from 14 to 17 produced a real external gain that survived giving the 14-predictor comparator its own hyperparameter search; further modeling did not, and the external plateau at ~0.77 survived every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly.
 151: 
 152: ### 4.2 Comparison With Prior Work
 153: Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014?2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ￣10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test R昌 of 33%?both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not?genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
 154: 
 155: ### 4.3 Strengths
 156: Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey?weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
 157: 
 158: ### 4.4 Limitations
 159: Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (＝3.1), which may bias the external estimates?especially the post-pandemic subgroup results?in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020?2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017?2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (＝2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (＝3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (＝3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14⊥17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (＝2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (＝3.6).
 160: 
 161: The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS?physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization?and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
 162: 
 163: ### 4.5 Conclusions
 164: A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set?most consistent with shift in the predictor?outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection?rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
