docs\prespecification_17var.md:3:**작성일: 2026-07-15 · 상태: 모델링 착수 전 확정(LOCKED BEFORE ANALYSIS)**
docs\cover_letter.md:11:**Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and—critically—that this ~0.77 external ceiling is a *concept-shift* limit intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, not a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman ρ≈0.97–0.99). A parsimonious 8-item version retains near-full external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool—squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
docs\cover_letter.md:13:**Methodological rigor and transparency.** The work follows TRIPOD+AI (2024; checklist enclosed as Multimedia Appendix 1) with PROBAST risk-of-bias self-assessment. We report a predictor-set amendment (14→17 predictors) that was documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; all model fitting, tuning, algorithm selection, and the choice of deployment item count used development-data cross-validation only, and the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search so the comparison is not confounded by tuning effort. We also disclose and correct three silent data-processing defects found in an adversarial data-integrity audit. The analysis code, variable-harmonization dictionary, model card, and protocol/pre-specification documents will be deposited in a public repository on acceptance; generative AI tools assisted with code and drafting under full author verification.
docs\model_card_17var.md:6:- **Inputs (17)**: age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, current drinking, physical-activity recommendation met, sleep hours, BMI, hypertension, diabetes, employment, self-rated oral health, food insecurity.
docs\model_card_17var.md:24:- Parsimonious 7-item deployment variant (order and item count selected entirely inside the training data): hold-out 0.819, external 0.760 (pre) and 0.758 (post) — a loss of ≤0.013 everywhere.
docs\model_card_17var.md:26:- Operating point (Youden): sensitivity/specificity ≈ 0.75/0.76 (internal), 0.76/0.65 (pre), 0.71/0.68 (post); PPV low (0.10–0.24), NPV high (0.95–0.99).
docs\model_card_17var.md:41:- **Food insecurity** is the most weakly harmonized predictor (single item vs 10-item module; 4–5× prevalence difference); results are robust to its removal (16-predictor model).
docs\model_card_17var.md:43:- Reproducibility: `17var_model/20_final_pipeline.py` (development, internal/external validation, parsimonious, sensitivity); `21_figures_tables.py` (SHAP, fairness, figures); `22_supplement.py`, `23_extra_metrics.py` (supplement); `41_audit_recompute.py`, `42_audit_tuning.py`, `43_consolidate.py` (verification re-runs). Results in `17var_model/results/final_*.csv`.
