docs\appendix2_crosswalk.md:90:Employment (`soa_01z1` ↔ `OCD150`), self-rated oral health (`ora_01z1` ↔ `OHQ845`), and household food insecurity (`nue_01z1` ↔ `FSDAD`) were the three concepts adopted. Harmonized definitions are given in Table A.2.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:17:**Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017–2025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES—14 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before analysis. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey–weighted with stratified cluster bootstrap CIs, plus SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017–March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021–2023; n=5,210) cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735–0.808; post 0.769, 0.739–0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). A 7-item model, with predictor order and item count fixed entirely inside the development data, lost ≤0.013 AUROC everywhere (0.819 internal, 0.760 pre, 0.758 post; H4 supported). Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an in-domain oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765–0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:21:**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:57:Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors—age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes—plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ↔ NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ↔ OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ↔ FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:59:**2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)–(iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature—food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ≈1.4) [13], unemployment (≈1.9–2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]—supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:63:**Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020–2021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (§2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (§2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:84:Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (≥5 as well as ≥10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (→14), removal of food insecurity only (→16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and—as pre-specified in the protocol—a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (annual drinking frequency, binge drinking, or all three together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (§2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ≥10): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates—e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively)—consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ≥20 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential—higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents—so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ≤0.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%→9.27% pre, 12.69%→12.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:123:To avoid advantaging the larger predictor set through tuning, the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search under the identical internal protocol (§2.8; best internal CV-AUROC 0.824). The three added predictors improved external discrimination over this independently tuned 14-predictor model: pre 0.749→**0.772** (Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; *P*<.001), post 0.753→**0.769** (Δ+0.016, 95% CI +0.008 to +0.023; *P*<.001); *P* values are from a paired rescaled cluster bootstrap with replicates centred on the null and a Monte-Carlo correction (2000 replicates), with internal AUROC rising 0.822→0.832. Independent tuning did not help the 14-predictor model—running it with the 17-predictor configuration gave nearly identical results (pre 0.750, post 0.754)—so the gain reflects predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Removing food insecurity alone (16-predictor model) retained most of the gain (pre 0.767, post 0.760), confirming the improvement does not depend on the single most weakly harmonized predictor.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:131:Predictor order and item count were both fixed inside the training set (§2.11). On the 50,000-record selection set, discrimination rose steeply to five items and then flattened: 1 item 0.753; 3 items 0.790; 5 items 0.820; 6 items 0.822; **7 items 0.829**; 8 items 0.832; 12 items 0.834; 17 items 0.835. Seven items was the smallest count within 0.01 AUROC of the full model on that selection set (0.829 vs 0.835; six items fell short at 0.822). Under the analysis plan's looser 0.02 margin the rule would have stopped at five items (0.820), which on evaluation gives hold-out 0.809 and external 0.742 (pre) and 0.746 (post); we deployed the stricter seven-item set because the five-item version loses about 0.03 externally.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:133:Evaluated afterwards on data never used for selection, the resulting 7-item model — self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, self-rated oral health, employment, and partnership — achieved hold-out AUROC **0.819**, NHANES pre **0.760** and NHANES post **0.758**, against 0.832/0.773/0.769 for the full 17-predictor model fitted on the same data. The loss is 0.013 internally, 0.012 pre-pandemic and 0.011 post-pandemic — **H4 supported**, with the caveat that the protocol described the tolerable loss qualitatively rather than as a numeric margin. Adding smoking as an eighth item raised pre-pandemic discrimination to 0.770 but lowered post-pandemic discrimination to 0.753, so the seven-item set is preferred for deployment. The full k=1–17 trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:139:Global SHAP importance was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.58), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.30), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.24)**, partnership (0.17), and **employment (0.17)**; food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 11th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top seven, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman ρ=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor–outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 11th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:143:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110–0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948–0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—and the 7-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.137 and 0.184; number needed to screen 7.3 and 5.4). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:156:Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey–weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:159:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (§3.1), which may bias the external estimates—especially the post-pandemic subgroup results—in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020–2021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017–2025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (§2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (§3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (§3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14→17 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (§2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (§3.6).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:164:A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set—most consistent with shift in the predictor–outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection—rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
