  71: ### 2.8 Model development
  72: Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations?main effects only, versus main effects plus four pre-specified interaction terms (age】self-rated health, self-rated health】oral health, age】employment, income】employment)?evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The primary model was selected by the rule fixed in the statistical analysis plan: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) fell inside the 0.005 tie band, so the pre-specified tie-break applied and selected LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest; elastic net (0.822) lay outside the band, and the parsimony criterion was never needed. LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
  73: 
  74: ### 2.9 Performance evaluation
  75: Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey?weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>?1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>?1) times the selection count (1000 replicates) [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
  76: 
  77: ### 2.10 External validation strategy
  78: Geographic: KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting. Temporal/COVID: evaluated separately in pre and post cycles. Recalibration: intercept/slope recalibration applied out-of-sample (cross-fit) and recovery quantified?updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7].
  79: 
  80: ### 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, deployment
  81: SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed in the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The analysis plan recommended keeping the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5?10 item ceiling; we applied a stricter 0.01 margin and report the 0.02 variant alongside it (∽3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (∽3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
  82: 
  83: ### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
  84: Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (∶5 as well as ∶10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (℃14), removal of food insecurity only (℃16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and?as pre-specified in the protocol?a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (annual drinking frequency, binge drinking, or all three together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (∽2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
  85: 
  86: ### 2.13 Data-integrity audit
  87: An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent ℃ constant imputation) ? corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN ? corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile ? corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.
  88: 
  89: ### 2.14 Ethical Considerations
  90: KCHS and NHANES are public, de-identified secondary datasets. NHANES data collection was approved by the NCHS Research Ethics Review Board, and KCHS is conducted under the authority of the KDCA. The present secondary analysis was reviewed by the Institutional Review Board of Daejeon University, which granted an exemption from review (approval number [to be inserted]) on the grounds that the study uses publicly available and de-identified data, collects and records no personal identifiers, and poses no more than minimal risk (no participant contact, intervention, or biological sampling). No re-identification was attempted, no compensation was involved, and no individually identifiable information is reported.
