  42: 
  43: ### 2.1 Study design and reporting standards
  44: Retrospective secondary-data study developing a clinical prediction model with external validation; reported per TRIPOD+AI (2024) [4], risk of bias self-assessed with PROBAST [5]. The study protocol and statistical analysis plan?including the four hypotheses?were written and dated before any analysis. The predictor-set expansion described in Ёз2.5 is a documented amendment written *after* the 14-predictor model had already been developed and applied to NHANES, and locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted (Ёз2.5.1). Multimedia Appendix 2 provides the year-specific harmonization crosswalk together with an English rendition of the locked amendment; the unmodified original protocol, statistical analysis plan, and amendment document will be deposited with the analysis code, hyperparameter grids, and model card in a public repository (DOI [to be inserted]; see Data Availability).
  45: 
  46: ### 2.2 Data sources
  47: **Development ? KCHS.** Nationwide KDCA survey of community-dwelling adults, annual in-person household interviews (~230,000/year), stratified/clustered/weighted complex-sample design. Pooled development sample: KCHS 2017?2025 with PHQ-9 administered; n=2,048,510. From this pooled sample, an outcome-stratified random split created a **500,000-record training set** and a **100,000-record internal hold-out set** used for all internal performance estimates; the remaining 1,448,510 records were not used. The training-set size was fixed for computational tractability before any external evaluation, and a subsequent check confirmed that expanding it toward the full pooled sample did not improve?and slightly reduced?external discrimination (Ёз3.5).
  48: **External validation ? US NHANES.** NCHS survey combining household interview and mobile examination center assessment; depression via the DPQ (PHQ-9) module. Two periods used as separate validation sets: pre-pandemic (2017?March 2020, "P_" files; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (August 2021?August 2023, "_L" files; n=5,210).
  49: 
  50: ### 2.3 Participants
  51: Adults ЁУ20 years with all nine PHQ-9 items answered. Pregnant respondents were retained in the main analysis. A prior physician diagnosis of depression is not recorded in the KCHS 2017?2025 questionnaires and therefore could not be used for exclusion or sensitivity analysis; this is noted as a limitation.
  52: 
  53: ### 2.4 Outcome
  54: Current depression = PHQ-9 total ЁУ10, computed identically in both surveys (nine items, 0?3). KCHS items recoded 1?4Ёц0?3 to match NHANES. Sensitivity: ЁУ5 cut-off.
  55: 
  56: ### 2.5 Candidate predictors and harmonization
  57: Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors?age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes?plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 Ёъ NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 Ёъ OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 Ёъ FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
  58: 
  59: **2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)?(iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature?food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ?1.4) [13], unemployment (?1.9?2.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]?supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
  60: 
  61: **Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)?(iii)?notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (ЅФAUROC <0.001; Ёз3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (Ёз3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.
  62: 
  63: **Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020?2021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (Ёз2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (Ёз2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
  64: 
  65: ### 2.6 Missing data
  66: All missing-data handling was performed by a preprocessing pipeline fitted in the development training set and applied unchanged to the internal hold-out and to each external set (no leakage). In the gradient-boosting primary model, binary predictors were passed through with missingness preserved and handled natively by the tree learner?so structurally missing binary items such as living-alone in NHANES pre are never imputed to a constant?while continuous predictors were median-imputed and categorical predictors mode-imputed within the same pipeline. For the linear comparator, continuous predictors were median-imputed, categorical predictors mode-imputed, and binary predictors mode-imputed with an added missing indicator. Household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health and self-rated oral health are ordinal scores and were modeled as numeric variables rather than as unordered categories, so they were median-imputed and standardized. Alternative strategies (missing indicators added for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors) were examined in sensitivity analysis (Ёз2.12). A silent-column-omission check was added after a data-integrity audit (see Ёз2.13).
