..\docs\protocol_text.md-7-Objective. To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ≥10) using the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and to externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital screening and triage tool.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:8:Methods. Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient-boosting models will be developed in KCHS using a harmonized predictor set shared with NHANES and evaluated for discrimination, calibration, and clinical utility (decision curve analysis), with SHAP-based interpretation and subgroup fairness. Geographic external validation will be performed in NHANES; temporal validation will contrast the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017–March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021–August 2023) cycles, with recalibration assessed. A parsimonious model will be derived for digital deployment.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-9-Results. This is a protocol; results are pending. Planned tables and figures, with pre-specified metrics and placeholders, are provided.
--
..\docs\protocol_text.md-15-Machine learning models for depression have been developed repeatedly from large national surveys, including the KCHS and the Korea National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, typically reporting strong discrimination. However, most published models are assessed only through internal validation (cross-validation or a hold-out split from the same survey). Internal performance is an optimistic indicator of real-world behavior: it does not establish that a model transports to a different sampling frame, health system, or population, and it does not test stability under temporal distribution shift.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:16:The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates (physical activity, sleep, social contact, employment) changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration—updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects—restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-17-This protocol addresses these gaps with a development-and-external-validation design that is unusually well suited to the outcome of interest. Depression is operationalized identically in both data sources: the KCHS and NHANES each administer the nine-item PHQ-9 with 0–3 scoring, so the outcome definition (sum ≥10) is common across surveys, minimizing outcome harmonization error. The two surveys differ in country, health system, racial/ethnic composition, and survey mode (household interview vs mobile examination center), making NHANES a substantively external validation set rather than a near-replicate. NHANES additionally provides National Center for Health Statistics-defined pre-pandemic (2017–March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021–August 2023) data, enabling temporal validation within the same external population.
--
