83-### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
84-Pre-specified and additional analyses (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7): outcome cut-off (≥5 as well as ≥10, evaluated internally and in both external cohorts), removal of the three added predictors (→14), removal of food insecurity only (→16), weighting approach (weighted vs unweighted training), alternative imputation (missing indicators for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of continuous predictors), an age lower bound of 30 years applied to the training *and* evaluation samples, and—as pre-specified in the protocol—a continuous-score analysis evaluating how the model ranks the untransformed PHQ-9 total, and alternative definitions of the alcohol predictor (annual drinking frequency, binge drinking, or all three together in place of current drinking). The self-report (KCHS) versus measured (NHANES) difference in BMI is inherent to the data sources and is addressed under harmonization cautions (§2.5) and Limitations rather than by swapping measurements. All analyses were performed in Python 3.14 (scikit-learn 1.9.0, lightgbm 4.6.0, shap 0.52.0, numpy 2.4.5, pandas 3.0.3, scipy 1.18.0, pyreadstat 1.3.5, matplotlib 3.10.9); complex-survey weighting, the stratified cluster bootstrap, calibration measures, and decision-curve net benefit were implemented directly rather than through a survey-analysis package, and the implementations are released with the code.
125:### 3.5 Why external discrimination plateaus at ~0.77: concept shift
126-Extensive leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments: feature engineering, hyperparameter search, heterogeneous ensembling, transfer-robust internal selection, domain adaptation, alternative losses, and structural variants) did not exceed external AUROC ~0.77 when model selection was restricted to KCHS-internal criteria. Apparent gains from externally-tuned variants (e.g., specific interaction terms, ensemble weights) did not survive leakage control.
127-
128-We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765–0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.757–0.787 by stratified cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is also estimated on the pooled pre+post sample, whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, so the comparison is approximate. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (−0.003 pre, −0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor–outcome relationship itself**—concept shift—although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (§2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (§3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
129-
130:### 3.6 Parsimonious deployment model
131-Predictor order and item count were both fixed inside the training set (§2.11). On the 50,000-record selection set, discrimination rose steeply to five items and then flattened: 1 item 0.753; 3 items 0.790; 5 items 0.820; 6 items 0.822; **7 items 0.829**; 8 items 0.832; 12 items 0.834; 17 items 0.835. Seven items was the smallest count within 0.01 AUROC of the full model on that selection set (0.829 vs 0.835; six items fell short at 0.822). Under the analysis plan's looser 0.02 margin the rule would have stopped at five items (0.820), which on evaluation gives hold-out 0.809 and external 0.742 (pre) and 0.746 (post); we deployed the stricter seven-item set because the five-item version loses about 0.03 externally.
132-
133-Evaluated afterwards on data never used for selection, the resulting 7-item model — self-rated health, age, sex, sleep duration, self-rated oral health, employment, and partnership — achieved hold-out AUROC **0.819**, NHANES pre **0.760** and NHANES post **0.758**, against 0.832/0.773/0.769 for the full 17-predictor model fitted on the same data. The loss is 0.013 internally, 0.012 pre-pandemic and 0.011 post-pandemic — **H4 supported**, with the caveat that the protocol described the tolerable loss qualitatively rather than as a numeric margin. Adding smoking as an eighth item raised pre-pandemic discrimination to 0.770 but lowered post-pandemic discrimination to 0.753, so the seven-item set is preferred for deployment. The full k=1–17 trajectory is tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S8.
134-
152:### 4.2 Comparison With Prior Work
153-Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014–2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ≥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test R² of 33%—both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not—genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
154-
155-### 4.3 Strengths
156-Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey–weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
docs/model_card_17var.md:4:- **Model**: Gradient-boosting classifier (LightGBM) predicting current depression (PHQ-9 ≥10).
docs/model_card_17var.md:8:- **Selection/tuning**: hyperparameters, feature representation, and algorithm all chosen by KCHS-internal cross-validation only (no external data used in any selection step). Selected config: num_leaves 31, min_child_samples 1000, learning_rate 0.01, n_estimators 900, reg_alpha 1.0, reg_lambda 20, colsample_bytree 0.6, subsample 0.6 (inactive — subsample_freq left at its default of 0, so no row subsampling occurs). Chosen from a 24-configuration randomized search × 2 feature representations by 4-fold survey-weighted CV (300,000-record subsample); algorithms compared at each one's tuned best under an identical 5-fold protocol (200,000-record subsample): LightGBM 0.840 > random forest 0.835 > elastic net 0.822.
docs/model_card_17var.md:13:- **Out of scope**: NOT a diagnostic device; not for standalone clinical decisions; not validated for individual-level clinical determination, for populations outside community-dwelling adults ≥20 years, or without local recalibration.
docs/model_card_17var.md:19:| Setting | AUROC (95% CI) | Calibration slope | After recalibration |
docs/model_card_17var.md:39:- **Deploy only with local recalibration**: discrimination transports, but calibration drifts across country and pandemic; intercept/slope recalibration on local data restores it.
docs/model_card_17var.md:40:- **Cross-national ceiling ~0.77**: external discrimination is bounded by concept shift (predictor–outcome relationships differ between Korea and the US); this is intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, confirmed by an in-domain oracle (~0.77) versus within-Korea performance (~0.83) and by >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments.
docs/cover_letter.md:11:**Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and—critically—that this ~0.77 external ceiling is a *concept-shift* limit intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, not a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman ρ≈0.97–0.99). A parsimonious 8-item version retains near-full external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool—squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:1:# Multimedia Appendix 4. Transportability-ceiling analyses
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:3:Supporting Results §3.5 (concept-shift ceiling) and Discussion §4.4 (development-survey coverage). All model selection used KCHS-internal criteria only. NHANES labels are used throughout for evaluation, as in any external validation; what is quarantined to the oracle rows is their use for model *fitting* (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only). Weighted AUROC throughout.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:5:## Table S2a. Leakage-controlled attempts to exceed the external ceiling
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:7:Every learner and adaptation was trained on KCHS and applied to NHANES without refitting (unless noted). None exceeded the pre-specified LightGBM baseline once selection was restricted to KCHS-internal information.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:11:| **LightGBM baseline (17 predictors, primary)** | **0.772** | **0.769** | reference |
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:20:| LightGBM + logistic blend (w=0.5) | 0.768 | 0.771 | offsetting |
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:44:Four neural architectures were trained on KCHS and transferred without refitting, with model/architecture selection by KCHS-internal validation only. All fell below gradient boosting internally (0.837) and none exceeded it on both external cohorts—consistent with the model-agnostic in-domain ceiling and with the established advantage of gradient boosting on modest-signal tabular data.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:66:The transported LightGBM model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) already meets the 17-predictor oracle, confirming the external number is at—not below—its achievable ceiling for this predictor set.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:70:Pooled pre+post NHANES, fitted and 5-fold cross-validated on its own labels; 95% CIs from the stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates, PSUs resampled within period-specific design strata). An in-sample (apparent) bound is not reported because it is strongly learner-dependent (0.77 for a heavily regularized learner, 0.83 for a more flexible one) and therefore carries no information about the ceiling.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:77:| LightGBM (small) | 0.772 | 0.757–0.787 |
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:81:- The 17-predictor external ceiling (~0.77) is a **concept-shift** limit: the transported model is already oracle-optimal for these predictors, and covariate-shift correction does not help (it lowers AUROC), indicating the predictor–outcome relationship, not the covariate distribution, differs across populations.
docs/appendix4_ceiling.md:82:- Multimorbidity and healthcare utilization carry additional, transportable signal in NHANES (oracle 0.765→0.778). These domains are **not collected by KCHS** (only hypertension and diabetes are recorded as physician-diagnosed conditions across all survey years, both already included), so they cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual ceiling is therefore development-survey coverage, not concept shift alone.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:17:**Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017–2025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES—14 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before analysis. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey–weighted with stratified cluster bootstrap CIs, plus SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017–March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021–2023; n=5,210) cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:19:**Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841), meeting the ≥0.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735–0.808; post 0.769, 0.739–0.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66–0.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749→0.772, Δ+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753→0.769, Δ+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). A 7-item model, with predictor order and item count fixed entirely inside the development data, lost ≤0.013 AUROC everywhere (0.819 internal, 0.760 pre, 0.758 post; H4 supported). Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an in-domain oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765–0.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81–0.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:21:**Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:33:The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction [2,10]. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration—updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7]—restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:37:We pursued four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in KCHS; (2) externally validate it in US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately pre/post-COVID-19 and quantify recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model for a low-burden digital tool. We hypothesized: **H1** the full model achieves internal AUROC ≥0.80; **H2** external discrimination remains clinically useful while calibration may deteriorate; **H3** recalibration substantially improves calibration; **H4** the parsimonious model incurs only a small performance loss.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:72:Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations—main effects only, versus main effects plus four pre-specified interaction terms (age×self-rated health, self-rated health×oral health, age×employment, income×employment)—evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The primary model was selected by the rule fixed in the statistical analysis plan: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) fell inside the 0.005 tie band, so the pre-specified tie-break applied and selected LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest; elastic net (0.822) lay outside the band, and the parsimony criterion was never needed. LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:75:Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-survey–weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>−1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>−1) times the selection count (1000 replicates) [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
docs/manuscript_17var.md:78:Geographic: KCHS-trained model applied to NHANES without refitting. Temporal/COVID: evaluated separately in pre and post cycles. Recalibration: intercept/slope recalibration applied out-of-sample (cross-fit) and recovery quantified—updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects [6,7].
docs/manuscript_17var.md:81:SHAP global importance [9] and cross-survey rank concordance of predictor importance (Spearman correlation between KCHS and each NHANES cycle) as a mechanistic view of transportability; fairness by weighted subgroup AUROC and calibration slope across sex, age, education, and (NHANES) race/ethnicity; and a parsimonious model for a low-burden self-administered form. Both the predictor order and the item count were determined entirely inside the development training set, which was split into a 450,000-record fitting set and a 50,000-record selection set: predictors were entered in descending order of global SHAP importance computed in the fitting set, refitted at every item count from 1 to 17, and the item count was chosen on selection-set AUROC. The analysis plan recommended keeping the smallest set within 0.02 AUROC of the full model, subject to a 5–10 item ceiling; we applied a stricter 0.01 margin and report the 0.02 variant alongside it (§3.6). The internal hold-out and both NHANES cohorts were used only for evaluation (§3.6). Positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a diagnostic.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:100:LightGBM was the primary model selected a priori by internal cross-validated AUROC (§2.8). Internal hold-out AUROC was **0.832 (95% CI 0.822–0.841)**, AUPRC 0.233, Brier 0.031, calibration slope 1.00, CITL +0.10 — **H1 met**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:103:See Table 2 and Figures 2 and 3. Discrimination remained clinically useful but lower: pre **0.772 (0.735–0.808)**, post **0.769 (0.739–0.799)**. Calibration deteriorated in both directions of the calibration line: the slope fell to 0.66 (pre) and 0.68 (post), and CITL rose to +0.49 and +1.11, indicating that the Korea-trained model systematically *under*-predicted absolute risk in the US—as expected from the large prevalence difference (KCHS 3.6% vs NHANES 8.4% and 12.2%). Out-of-sample intercept/slope recalibration restored calibration (slope→0.99, CITL→0.00) with discrimination essentially unchanged — **H2 and H3 supported**.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:105:**Table 2. Discrimination and calibration (primary model, LightGBM, 17 predictors, weighted).**
docs/manuscript_17var.md:118:CIs are from a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates; §2.9). CITL is the offset-model calibration intercept, so positive values denote under-prediction of observed risk. Expected calibration error (ECE) confirms excellent internal calibration (0.004) with drift on external application (0.032 pre, 0.067 post), consistent with the calibration pattern and recoverable by recalibration.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:120:Recalibration is intercept/slope updating applied out-of-sample (5-fold cross-fit); because each fold uses a separate monotone calibration map, the recalibrated AUROC may differ trivially (by ≤0.003) from the original and is not the target of recalibration (calibration slope/CITL are). Internal calibration uses the original probabilities. Race/ethnicity is used only for the NHANES fairness analysis, not as a predictor.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:128:We localized the ceiling with an **in-domain oracle**: fitting NHANES on its own labels (a deliberate §2.10 violation, for diagnosis only) under cross-validation. Across logistic, penalized-logistic, histogram-gradient-boosting and LightGBM learners, the honest 5-fold cross-validated oracle reached only 0.765–0.772 (best learner 0.772, 95% CI 0.757–0.787 by stratified cluster bootstrap). The KCHS-transferred model (pre 0.772, post 0.769) therefore already **matches the best in-domain performance any of these learners achieved when fitted directly to NHANES**. This is an empirical benchmark over the learners we tried, not a proof of a maximum; it is also estimated on the pooled pre+post sample, whereas the transported figures are cycle-specific, so the comparison is approximate. Matching sample size does not close the gap: reducing the Korean data to the NHANES sample size and cross-validating within it still yielded 0.823 at n=7,888 (the pre-pandemic cohort size) and 0.810 at n=13,098 (the pooled oracle size), rising to 0.830 only at n=200,000 (best of three learners, 5-fold weighted AUROC). At identical n, in other words, the same 17 predictors carry 0.81–0.82 of discrimination in Korea against an NHANES ceiling of ~0.77. Three further checks converged on the same conclusion: (i) covariate-shift correction by density-ratio reweighting of the KCHS training sample toward the NHANES covariate distribution (labels unused) *reduced* external AUROC (to 0.766/0.758), indicating the gap is not covariate shift; (ii) increasing the KCHS training set from 0.5M to 2M examples did not raise—and slightly lowered—external discrimination, consistent with source-specific overfitting rather than an estimation-limited ceiling; and (iii) adding the remaining harmonized-but-unused survey items did not help: public-assistance receipt alone left external AUROC unchanged (0.772/0.769), and adding all nine remaining candidates together slightly *reduced* it (−0.003 pre, −0.001 post in the exploration run). Deep tabular learners (a multilayer perceptron, TabNet, and an FT-Transformer) were additionally trained and transferred; all fell below gradient boosting internally and none exceeded it on both external cohorts (Multimedia Appendix 4), consistent with the model-agnostic ceiling. Sample size, model choice, covariate imbalance and omitted harmonized information were each tested and none accounts for the gap. The most consistent remaining explanation is **shift in the predictor–outcome relationship itself**—concept shift—although the surveys also differ in how several predictors are measured (§2.5) and the external cohorts are complete-case samples with differential depression-module non-response (§3.1), so measurement and selection effects cannot be separated from concept shift with these data. What the evidence does establish is that the gap is not closable by recalibration, which corrects calibration rather than rank ordering, nor by more data or a different learner.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:143:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110–0.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948–0.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)—a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4—and the 7-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.137 and 0.184; number needed to screen 7.3 and 5.4). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies—screening everyone and screening no one—at threshold probabilities of 0.10–0.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4–S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:150:A depression-screening ML model developed in Korea and externally validated in the US met its internal target (AUROC 0.832) and retained clinically useful—though lower—discrimination (~0.77) on genuinely external US data and across the COVID-19 shift, with calibration recoverable by lightweight recalibration. Expanding the transportable predictor set from 14 to 17 produced a real external gain that survived giving the 14-predictor comparator its own hyperparameter search; further modeling did not, and the external plateau at ~0.77 survived every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:153:Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014–2018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ≥10 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test R² of 33%—both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not—genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:156:Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey–weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:161:The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS—physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization—and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
docs/manuscript_17var.md:164:A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set—most consistent with shift in the predictor–outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection—rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
