15: **Objective.** To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ??0) in the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital triage tool.
16: 
17: **Methods.** Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting were developed in KCHS (2017??025; pooled n=2,048,510; outcome-stratified 500,000-record training set and 100,000-record hold-out) using a harmonized 17-predictor set shared with NHANES??4 protocol-specified predictors plus employment, oral health, and food insecurity added by a documented amendment locked before analysis. All algorithm, feature-representation and hyperparameter selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only; the trained model was applied to NHANES without refitting. Evaluation was complex-survey?“weighted with stratified cluster bootstrap CIs, plus SHAP interpretation and subgroup fairness. Temporal validation contrasted the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017?“March 2020; n=7,888) and post-pandemic (2021??023; n=5,210) cycles, with intercept/slope recalibration.
18: 
19: **Results.** Gradient boosting (LightGBM) was the primary model. Internal AUROC was 0.832 (95% CI 0.822??.841), meeting the ??.80 target (H1). External discrimination remained clinically useful but lower (pre 0.772, 95% CI 0.735??.808; post 0.769, 0.739??.799); calibration deteriorated (slope 0.66??.68; calibration-in-the-large +0.49 and +1.11, ie, systematic under-prediction) and was fully restored by recalibration (slope??.99, CITL??.00; H2, H3 supported). Against an independently tuned 14-predictor model, the three added predictors improved external discrimination (pre 0.749??.772, ?+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; post 0.753??.769, ?+0.016, +0.008 to +0.023; both *P*<.001). A 7-item model, with predictor order and item count fixed entirely inside the development data, lost ??.013 AUROC everywhere (0.819 internal, 0.760 pre, 0.758 post; H4 supported). Leakage-controlled optimization (>450 experiments) did not exceed ~0.77 externally, and an in-domain oracle fitted directly to NHANES reached only 0.765??.772, whereas the same predictors reached 0.81??.82 in equally sized Korean samples.
20: 
21: **Conclusions.** A Korea-developed depression screener transported to the US and across the pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and fully recoverable calibration. The cross-national ceiling (~0.77) persisted against sample size, algorithm choice, covariate-shift correction and an in-domain oracle, and is therefore a property of the transportable predictor set rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
22: 
23: **Keywords:** depression; mass screening; machine learning; supervised machine learning; models, statistical; external validation; transportability; concept shift; COVID-19; mobile health; Patient Health Questionnaire; Republic of Korea.
