56: ### 2.5 Candidate predictors and harmonization
57: Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors?”age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes?”plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ??NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ??OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ??FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
58: 
59: **2.5.1 Predictor-set amendment and selection provenance.** The three added predictors were identified by crosswalking all 616 KCHS concepts against the NHANES codebook for both cycles (a 175-variable extract of candidate items at the time of the amendment; a later, exhaustive extraction identified 265 variables common to both cycles). The amendment was locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, but not before the study as a whole: the 14-predictor model had already been developed and transferred, and the locked document explicitly records the then-known internal AUROC (0.824) and the then-observed external level (~0.75) when stating its expectations (Multimedia Appendix 2). The expansion decision therefore could not be tuned on 17-predictor external results, but it was not blind to how the 14-predictor model had performed externally, and we report it as a documented amendment rather than as a blinded pre-registration. The amendment required a candidate to be (i) available in both NHANES cycles, (ii) collected in more than one KCHS survey year, and (iii) not circular with the PHQ-9 outcome. Employment, self-rated oral health, and household food insecurity were the three concepts adopted. Candidates rejected at this stage failed on single-year collection (e.g., dyslipidemia, osteoporosis, arthritis), concept mismatch (KCHS records only *awareness of early warning signs* for stroke and myocardial infarction, not physician diagnosis), or absence from the NHANES pre-pandemic cycle (functional limitation). Criteria (i)??iii) themselves use survey documentation and variable availability only, and no NHANES outcome association was estimated for any candidate before the amendment was locked. Each added predictor has an established association with depression in the epidemiological literature?”food insecurity (pooled odds ratio ??.4) [13], unemployment (??.9??.1) [14], and poor oral health (a bidirectional relationship) [15]?”supporting biological and social plausibility rather than data-driven selection. Circular/outcome-contaminated variables (PHQ items, suicidal ideation, EQ-5D anxiety/depression, happiness, perceived stress, mental-health service use including NHANES HUQ090) were pre-specified for exclusion.
60: 
61: **Post-hoc candidate screening (reported for completeness).** The later exhaustive extraction surfaced further harmonizable items, some of which would also have satisfied criteria (i)??iii)?”notably public-assistance receipt, alcohol frequency, alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep duration; the alcohol and sleep items are refinements of predictors already in the set. After the primary analysis was complete we screened all of them, comparing the direction of their adjusted odds ratios with depression across KCHS, NHANES-pre, and NHANES-post, and then testing them in the model. Alcohol quantity, binge drinking, and weekend sleep reversed direction between countries; public-assistance receipt was directionally consistent but added no external discrimination when included (?AUROC <0.001; ยง3.5). No item was added, so the predictor set is unchanged from the locked amendment. Because this screening inspected NHANES outcome associations, it is reported as a post-hoc diagnostic and was not used for selection. Two robustness analyses further bound the amendment's contribution: the 14-predictor protocol-specified model is reported alongside the 17-predictor model throughout, and a food-insecurity leave-out (16-predictor) shows the incremental gain does not rest on the most weakly harmonized predictor (ยง3.4). Model fitting, hyperparameter tuning, and primary-model selection used KCHS-internal cross-validation only, with no NHANES data.
62: 
63: **Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020??021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (ยง2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (ยง2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
64: 
65: ### 2.6 Missing data
