118: CIs are from a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap (1000 replicates; ยง2.9). CITL is the offset-model calibration intercept, so positive values denote under-prediction of observed risk. Expected calibration error (ECE) confirms excellent internal calibration (0.004) with drift on external application (0.032 pre, 0.067 post), consistent with the calibration pattern and recoverable by recalibration.
119: 
120: Recalibration is intercept/slope updating applied out-of-sample (5-fold cross-fit); because each fold uses a separate monotone calibration map, the recalibrated AUROC may differ trivially (by ??.003) from the original and is not the target of recalibration (calibration slope/CITL are). Internal calibration uses the original probabilities. Race/ethnicity is used only for the NHANES fairness analysis, not as a predictor.
121: 
122: ### 3.4 Effect of the added predictors
123: To avoid advantaging the larger predictor set through tuning, the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search under the identical internal protocol (ยง2.8; best internal CV-AUROC 0.824). The three added predictors improved external discrimination over this independently tuned 14-predictor model: pre 0.749??*0.772** (?+0.023, 95% CI +0.013 to +0.033; *P*<.001), post 0.753??*0.769** (?+0.016, 95% CI +0.008 to +0.023; *P*<.001); *P* values are from a paired rescaled cluster bootstrap with replicates centred on the null and a Monte-Carlo correction (2000 replicates), with internal AUROC rising 0.822??.832. Independent tuning did not help the 14-predictor model?”running it with the 17-predictor configuration gave nearly identical results (pre 0.750, post 0.754)?”so the gain reflects predictor content rather than modeling strategy. Removing food insecurity alone (16-predictor model) retained most of the gain (pre 0.767, post 0.760), confirming the improvement does not depend on the single most weakly harmonized predictor.
145: ---
146: 
147: ## 4. Discussion
148: 
149: ### 4.1 Principal Findings
150: A depression-screening ML model developed in Korea and externally validated in the US met its internal target (AUROC 0.832) and retained clinically useful?”though lower?”discrimination (~0.77) on genuinely external US data and across the COVID-19 shift, with calibration recoverable by lightweight recalibration. Expanding the transportable predictor set from 14 to 17 produced a real external gain that survived giving the 14-predictor comparator its own hyperparameter search; further modeling did not, and the external plateau at ~0.77 survived every leakage-controlled attempt to raise it, including matching sample size, correcting covariate shift and fitting NHANES directly.
151: 
152: ### 4.2 Comparison With Prior Work
153: Prior ML depression models built on Korean national surveys report strong internal performance but no external validation. Using KNHANES 2014??018 restricted to non-smokers at risk of second-hand smoke exposure (n=11,463), Kim et al [12] classified PHQ-9 ??0 with internal AUROC up to 0.90; using the 2023 KCHS restricted to culturally diverse families (n=2,568), Kim et al [11] predicted the continuous PHQ-9 score with a test Rยฒ of 33%?”both identifying perceived stress and subjective health as dominant predictors, and neither attempting external validation. Two deliberate design choices explain why our internal discrimination is lower (0.83): we excluded outcome-proximal predictors such as perceived stress and single-item depressed-mood questions, which inflate apparent performance but are near-tautological with the outcome; and we restricted the predictor set to variables transportable to a second country. In exchange, this study provides what prior work did not?”genuine cross-national and cross-pandemic external validation, an explicit transportability ceiling characterized by an in-domain oracle and >450 leakage-controlled experiments, and a recalibration analysis. That the same predictors carry substantially more signal within Korea (~0.83) than in the US (~0.77) is a concrete instance of the dataset-shift problem described for deployed clinical AI [10], and underscores that high internally-reported AUROCs do not guarantee cross-population usefulness.
154: 
155: ### 4.3 Strengths
156: Identical PHQ-9 outcome; genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode; temporal validation with recalibration; complex-survey?“weighted evaluation with stratified cluster CIs; a predictor-set amendment documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; equal-effort tuning of the three candidate algorithms and of the 14-predictor comparator; a data-integrity audit; and leakage-controlled optimization that rules out overfitting-driven optimism.
157: 
158: ### 4.4 Limitations
159: Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (ยง3.1), which may bias the external estimates?”especially the post-pandemic subgroup results?”in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020??021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017??025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (ยง2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (ยง3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (ยง3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14??7 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (ยง2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (ยง3.6).
160: 
161: The transportable predictor set is also bounded by the *development* survey's content. In a coverage analysis we extracted candidate domains available in NHANES but not harmonizable to KCHS?”physician-diagnosed multimorbidity (asthma, arthritis, thyroid, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, liver disease, and cancer, excluding circulatory conditions) and healthcare utilization?”and found that adding them raised the NHANES in-domain oracle from 0.765 to 0.778. KCHS, however, records only hypertension and diabetes among physician-diagnosed conditions (both already included) and does not collect a broader chronic-disease inventory, hospitalization, or visit counts; these domains therefore cannot enter a KCHS-developed transportable model. Part of the residual external gap is thus attributable to development-survey coverage rather than concept shift alone, and a development survey that captured multimorbidity and utilization could plausibly lift the transportable ceiling by ~0.01 AUROC.
162: 
163: ### 4.5 Conclusions
164: A survey-derived depression screener transports across country and pandemic with clinically useful discrimination and recoverable calibration. The cross-national discrimination ceiling (~0.77) is a property of the transportable predictor set?”most consistent with shift in the predictor?“outcome relationship, with residual contributions from differing measurement, development-survey coverage and complete-case selection?”rather than a modeling failure. A low-burden 7-item version, paired with local recalibration, is a plausible scalable digital triage aid.
165: 
