97:Weighted depression prevalence (PHQ-9 ??0): KCHS 3.6%, NHANES pre 8.4%, post 12.2% (Figure 1). The surveys differed markedly on measured covariates?”e.g., mean BMI 23.7 (KCHS) vs 29.8 (NHANES), and weighted food-insecurity prevalence 3.7% in KCHS vs 16.4% and 17.2% in the NHANES cycles, computed among respondents with the item recorded (missing 22.1%, 6.0% and 6.3%, respectively)?”consistent with genuinely distinct populations and, for food insecurity, with the single-item-vs-module measurement difference. DPQ item non-response was substantial in both NHANES cycles and much larger after the pandemic: among adults ??0 years, 14.3% of the pre-pandemic and 32.7% of the post-pandemic respondents left three or more PHQ-9 items unanswered, yielding the complete-case analytic samples of 7,888 and 5,210 (item-level detail in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S10). Non-response was differential?”higher among respondents with less than high-school education (20.6% pre, 47.3% post) and among Asian and Black respondents?”so the external cohorts, and particularly the post-pandemic fairness estimates, should be read as complete-case results. Reassuringly, adding respondents missing only one or two items using their person-mean changed the observed prevalence by ??.1 percentage points in both cycles (unweighted: 9.23%??.27% pre, 12.69%??2.78% post). Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6.
136:Discrimination was stable across specifications that alter modeling choices rather than the target condition (Figure 4): weighted training (0.831/0.773/0.764 internal/pre/post), missing-indicator imputation (0.833/0.770/0.767), single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors (0.833/0.770/0.767), and the 16- and 14-predictor variants (Â§3.4) all lay within 0.01 of the primary model internally. Restricting both training and evaluation to respondents aged ??0 years *improved* discrimination (0.840/0.784/0.788), consistent with the lower subgroup performance observed at ages 20??9 (Â§3.8). Changing the outcome definition to PHQ-9 ?? lowered discrimination throughout (0.759/0.736/0.739) at a much higher weighted prevalence (16.1%/25.0%/32.1%), as expected when the target shifts from moderate-or-worse symptoms to any symptom burden; notably, the Korea?“US gap narrows from about 0.06 AUROC at the ??0 threshold to about 0.02 at ?? (0.759 vs 0.736??.739), suggesting that the transportability loss is concentrated at the more severe end of the symptom distribution. Replacing current drinking with annual drinking frequency, with binge drinking, or entering all three together?”the alternative alcohol definitions listed in the analysis plan?”changed discrimination by at most 0.003 externally (pre 0.769??.772, post 0.769??.770). In the pre-specified continuous-score analysis, the deployed classifier ranked the untransformed PHQ-9 total with weighted Spearman ?=0.378 internally, 0.403 pre-pandemic and 0.439 post-pandemic, and a model trained directly on the continuous score performed similarly (?=0.402/0.403/0.424; weighted RÂ² 0.216/0.166/0.102), so the transportability pattern is not an artifact of dichotomizing the outcome. Data-integrity fixes (Â§2.13) did not alter substantive conclusions. Exact values are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7.
139:Global SHAP importance was dominated by self-rated health (mean |SHAP| 0.58), followed by age (0.38), sex (0.30), sleep duration (0.26), **self-rated oral health (0.24)**, partnership (0.17), and **employment (0.17)**; food insecurity contributed modestly (0.06, ranked 11th). Two of the three added predictors (oral health, employment) thus rank within the top seven, consistent with their measured incremental value (Figure 5). The rank ordering of predictor importance was highly concordant between KCHS and both NHANES cycles (Spearman ?=0.97 pre, 0.99 post), indicating that the two populations use the predictors in a similar order; this suggests the transportability limit lies in the *strength*, not the *structure*, of predictor?“outcome associations. The clearest exception was food insecurity, which ranked 11th in KCHS but 7th in NHANES pre, consistent with its different measurement (single item vs 10-item module) and much higher prevalence in the US.
141:Subgroup discrimination in KCHS was fairly uniform (weighted AUROC 0.785??.859): sex (female 0.822, 95% CI 0.811??.833; male 0.831, 0.816??.845), education (0.813??.830), and age (20??9 years 0.785, 0.766??.802 < 40??9 years 0.828, 0.812??.843 < ??0 years 0.859, 0.849??.870). In NHANES, discrimination was lower and far less precisely estimated. Pre-pandemic: Mexican American 0.697 (0.625??.777; n=914), non-Hispanic Black 0.762 (0.727??.800; n=2,096), other/multiracial 0.772 (0.673??.871; n=381), other Hispanic 0.777 (0.733??.818; n=813), non-Hispanic White 0.778 (0.727??.825; n=2,798), non-Hispanic Asian 0.810 (0.725??.875; n=886). Post-pandemic: Mexican American 0.689 (0.609??.794; n=330), non-Hispanic Black 0.727 (0.650??.794; n=598), non-Hispanic White 0.770 (0.723??.812; n=3,215), other/multiracial 0.786 (0.709??.873; n=340), other Hispanic 0.790 (0.747??.843; n=499), non-Hispanic Asian 0.841 (0.638??.988; n=228). These intervals are wide and mutually overlapping, and all but the Mexican American interval overlap the cohort-level interval, so the ordering should not be read as established differential performance; that the lowest point estimate falls in Mexican American respondents in both cycles nonetheless warrants monitoring as an equity caveat (Figure 6). Internal subgroup calibration was good (slope 0.97??.02 across sex and age strata, 0.93??.03 including education strata; subgroup CITL ??.02 to +0.26). In NHANES the subgroup calibration slopes were uniformly below 1 (0.55??.78, one exception) and subgroup CITL was positive in every group except Mexican American pre-pandemic (up to +1.30), confirming that the external miscalibration is under-prediction of absolute risk and not only over-extreme ranking (Â§3.3). Exact subgroup values with CIs are tabulated in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S9.
143:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110??.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948??.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)?”a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4?”and the 7-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.137 and 0.184; number needed to screen 7.3 and 5.4). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies?”screening everyone and screening no one?”at threshold probabilities of 0.10??.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4?“S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
194:**Figure legends.** Figure 1: Weighted sample characteristics across cohorts?”panel A, prevalence/proportion; panel B, means (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S6). Figure 2: Calibration plots (internal, NHANES pre, NHANES post; original probabilities). Figure 3: Receiver operating characteristic curves (weighted) for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post. Figure 4: Sensitivity analyses?”weighted AUROC across specifications for internal, NHANES pre, and NHANES post (exact values in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S7). Figure 5: Global SHAP importance of the 17 predictors (KCHS). Figure 6: Subgroup discrimination (weighted AUROC with 95% CIs) by sex, age, and education (KCHS hold-out) and by race/ethnicity (NHANES pre and post). Figure 7: Decision curves (weighted net benefit) for the three datasets, each with its own screen-all and screen-none default strategies; original, non-recalibrated probabilities. Tables: Table 1 (harmonized predictor set) and Table 2 (discrimination and calibration).

