..\docs\protocol_text.md-6-Background. Depression is a leading contributor to the global burden of disease, and scalable, low-cost screening is needed in community and primary-care settings. Machine learning (ML) prediction models built on national health surveys are common, but most are evaluated only with internal validation. Evidence on whether such models transport to a different country, health system, and population, and whether they remain stable across the distribution shift caused by the COVID-19 pandemic, is scarce.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-7-Objective. To develop an ML model for current depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9 [PHQ-9] ??0) using the Korea Community Health Survey (KCHS) and to externally validate it in the US National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) across the pre- and post-COVID-19 periods, as the engine for a deployable digital screening and triage tool.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:8:Methods. Penalized logistic regression, random forest, and gradient-boosting models will be developed in KCHS using a harmonized predictor set shared with NHANES and evaluated for discrimination, calibration, and clinical utility (decision curve analysis), with SHAP-based interpretation and subgroup fairness. Geographic external validation will be performed in NHANES; temporal validation will contrast the NCHS-defined pre-pandemic (2017?“March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021?“August 2023) cycles, with recalibration assessed. A parsimonious model will be derived for digital deployment.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-9-Results. This is a protocol; results are pending. Planned tables and figures, with pre-specified metrics and placeholders, are provided.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-10-Conclusions. The study will quantify the cross-national and cross-pandemic transportability of a survey-based depression screening model and assess its suitability as a low-burden digital triage tool.
--
..\docs\protocol_text.md-14-Depression is among the leading causes of years lived with disability worldwide, and its detection at the community and primary-care level remains incomplete. Brief self-report instruments such as the PHQ-9 enable population-scale screening, yet applying a fixed cut-off uniformly across heterogeneous populations does not prioritize those at highest risk. Risk-stratification tools that combine routinely available sociodemographic and behavioral information could support more efficient triage, and, when delivered through web or mobile interfaces, could scale at low marginal cost?”an application squarely within the scope of internet-based health services.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-15-Machine learning models for depression have been developed repeatedly from large national surveys, including the KCHS and the Korea National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey, typically reporting strong discrimination. However, most published models are assessed only through internal validation (cross-validation or a hold-out split from the same survey). Internal performance is an optimistic indicator of real-world behavior: it does not establish that a model transports to a different sampling frame, health system, or population, and it does not test stability under temporal distribution shift.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:16:The COVID-19 pandemic constitutes a natural distribution shift for mental-health prediction. Population distributions of depressive symptoms and of their behavioral correlates (physical activity, sleep, social contact, employment) changed during the pandemic, and a model trained before 2020 may show degraded calibration afterward even if discrimination is preserved. Whether recalibration?”updating only the intercept and slope without re-estimating predictor effects?”restores clinical usefulness is a practically important, under-studied question for deployed screening tools.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-17-This protocol addresses these gaps with a development-and-external-validation design that is unusually well suited to the outcome of interest. Depression is operationalized identically in both data sources: the KCHS and NHANES each administer the nine-item PHQ-9 with 0?? scoring, so the outcome definition (sum ??0) is common across surveys, minimizing outcome harmonization error. The two surveys differ in country, health system, racial/ethnic composition, and survey mode (household interview vs mobile examination center), making NHANES a substantively external validation set rather than a near-replicate. NHANES additionally provides National Center for Health Statistics-defined pre-pandemic (2017?“March 2020) and pandemic/post-pandemic (August 2021?“August 2023) data, enabling temporal validation within the same external population.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:18:We therefore pursue four aims: (1) develop an ML depression-screening model in the KCHS; (2) externally validate it in the US NHANES (geographic transportability); (3) validate it separately in the pre- and post-COVID-19 NHANES cycles and quantify the effect of recalibration (temporal transportability); and (4) derive a parsimonious model suitable for a low-burden digital self-screening and triage tool. We hypothesize that the full model will achieve an AUROC of at least 0.80 internally; that discrimination will remain clinically useful on external validation while calibration may deteriorate; that recalibration will significantly improve calibration; and that the parsimonious model will incur only a small, clinically negligible loss of performance.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-19-
..\docs\protocol_text.md-20-[Heading1] 2. Methods
