..\docs\protocol_text.md-133-[ListParagraph] Geographic: the KCHS-trained model is applied to NHANES without refitting.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-134-[ListParagraph] Temporal/COVID: evaluation is repeated separately in NHANES pre (2017?“March 2020) and post (August 2021?“August 2023).
..\docs\protocol_text.md:135:[ListParagraph] Recalibration: if calibration degrades, intercept/slope recalibration (or Platt/temperature scaling) is applied and the recovery quantified?”updating calibration only, without re-estimating predictor effects (a deployment-friendly update).
..\docs\protocol_text.md-136-[Heading2] 2.11 Interpretability, fairness, and deployment
..\docs\protocol_text.md-137-SHAP values summarize global and local predictor contributions, and the rank concordance of top predictors between KCHS and NHANES is examined as a mechanistic view of transportability. Fairness is assessed by reporting AUROC, calibration, and error rates across sex, age, education, income, and (in NHANES) race/ethnicity. For deployment, a parsimonious model using the top 5??0 predictors is derived to minimize item burden in a self-administered web/mobile form; a conceptual triage workflow routes low-risk users to self-management resources and higher-risk users to professional assessment. The tool is positioned as a screening/triage aid, not a standalone diagnostic or clinical-decision system.
--
..\docs\protocol_text.md-183-Candidate algorithms will be compared by cross-validated AUROC/AUPRC and calibration; the primary model will be selected a priori by pre-specified criteria. Internal discrimination is expected in the AUROC ??[0.80??.88] range based on prior survey-based models. Reported: internal AUROC [ ], AUPRC [ ], calibration slope [ ], Brier [ ].
..\docs\protocol_text.md-184-[Heading2] 3.3 Geographic and temporal (COVID) external validation
..\docs\protocol_text.md:185:Discrimination, calibration, and clinical utility in NHANES pre- and post-COVID cycles, before and after recalibration, are reported in Table 4, with calibration plots (Figure 2) and decision curves (Figure 3). We anticipate preserved discrimination with possible calibration drift that recalibration substantially corrects.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-186-Table 4. Discrimination, calibration, and operating characteristics (shell).
