..\docs\protocol_text.md-228-[Heading1] 4. Discussion
..\docs\protocol_text.md-229-[Heading2] 4.1 Principal findings (anticipated)
..\docs\protocol_text.md:230:This study will provide, to our knowledge, one of the first evaluations of a depression-screening ML model that is developed in one country?™s national survey and externally validated in another country?™s survey using an identically defined PHQ-9 outcome, while simultaneously testing stability across the COVID-19 distribution shift. The design directly targets the transportability question that internal validation cannot answer, and it clarifies whether lightweight recalibration is sufficient to maintain clinical usefulness after a population-level shock.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-231-[Heading2] 4.2 Comparison with prior work
..\docs\protocol_text.md-232-Prior KCHS- and KNHANES-based ML depression models generally report internal validation only; cross-national external validation and explicit pre/post-COVID temporal validation are rare. By harmonizing predictors to the intersection of the two surveys and using an identical outcome, this protocol reduces the outcome- and label-shift confounding that complicates cross-dataset comparisons.
--
..\docs\protocol_text.md-234-[ListParagraph] identical PHQ-9 outcome across development and validation, minimizing outcome harmonization error.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-235-[ListParagraph] genuinely external validation across country, health system, race/ethnicity, and survey mode.
..\docs\protocol_text.md:236:[ListParagraph] temporal validation across NCHS-defined pre/post-COVID periods with recalibration analysis.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-237-[ListParagraph] interpretability, fairness, clinical-utility (DCA), and a deployment-oriented parsimonious model, aligned with digital-health translation.
..\docs\protocol_text.md-238-[Heading2] 4.4 Limitations
