docs\manuscript_17var.md:57:Only variables definable identically in both surveys entered the harmonized model. The final harmonized set comprised **17 predictors** (Table 1): the 14 protocol-specified predictors?”age, sex, education, household income (within-survey quintiles), self-rated health, partnership, living alone, smoking, alcohol, physical activity, sleep duration, BMI, hypertension, diabetes?”plus three added predictors: **employment** (KCHS soa_01z1 ??NHANES OCD150), **self-rated oral health** (ora_01z1 ??OHQ845), and **household food insecurity** (nue_01z1 ??FSDAD). Concept-level harmonization used a label-based per-year crosswalk on the KCHS side and cycle-specific code branching on the NHANES side; self-rated health was harmonized to NHANES HUQ010 (correcting a Table 1 file-code error for HSD010/HSQ).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:63:**Harmonization cautions.** Alcohol, physical activity, and income use different instruments and were reduced to conservatively comparable definitions (residuals quantified in sensitivity). BMI is self-reported in KCHS, measured in NHANES. Living-alone is unavailable in the NHANES pre-pandemic DEMO file (100% missing) and is retained as missing (not imputed to a constant) to avoid fabricating a constant. Several KCHS items are also structurally missing for whole survey years because the module rotates: food insecurity was not fielded in 2020??021 (22.1% missing overall), sleep duration was not fielded in 2023 (11.3%), and BMI could be neither read nor derived in 2019 (13.1%). These gaps are year-structural rather than respondent-level, are preserved as missing rather than imputed to a constant, and are addressed by the missing-data strategy (ยง2.6) and by leave-out and imputation sensitivity analyses (ยง2.12). Food insecurity additionally uses a single KCHS item versus the 10-item NHANES module. The full year-by-year variable map is provided as Multimedia Appendix 2.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:66:All missing-data handling was performed by a preprocessing pipeline fitted in the development training set and applied unchanged to the internal hold-out and to each external set (no leakage). In the gradient-boosting primary model, binary predictors were passed through with missingness preserved and handled natively by the tree learner?”so structurally missing binary items such as living-alone in NHANES pre are never imputed to a constant?”while continuous predictors were median-imputed and categorical predictors mode-imputed within the same pipeline. For the linear comparator, continuous predictors were median-imputed, categorical predictors mode-imputed, and binary predictors mode-imputed with an added missing indicator. Household income (within-survey quintile), self-rated health and self-rated oral health are ordinal scores and were modeled as numeric variables rather than as unordered categories, so they were median-imputed and standardized. Alternative strategies (missing indicators added for continuous and binary predictors; single chained-equations imputation of the continuous predictors) were examined in sensitivity analysis (ยง2.12). A silent-column-omission check was added after a data-integrity audit (see ยง2.13).
docs\manuscript_17var.md:72:Algorithms: penalized logistic regression (elastic net), random forest, and gradient boosting (LightGBM). Development proceeded in two selection steps, both confined to the KCHS training set. First, the gradient-boosting hyperparameters and the feature representation were chosen by a randomized search over 24 LightGBM configurations crossed with two representations?”main effects only, versus main effects plus four pre-specified interaction terms (ageร—self-rated health, self-rated healthร—oral health, ageร—employment, incomeร—employment)?”evaluated by 4-fold survey-weighted cross-validation in a 300,000-record subsample of the training set; the main-effects-only representation won, and adding interaction terms did not improve cross-validated discrimination. Second, all three algorithms were tuned over comparable 5-configuration grids under an identical protocol (5-fold stratified cross-validation in a 200,000-record subsample of the training set, scored by unweighted cross-validated AUROC) and compared at each algorithm's best configuration, so that no algorithm was advantaged by differential tuning effort. The primary model was selected by the rule fixed in the statistical analysis plan: highest cross-validated AUROC, with differences smaller than 0.005 treated as ties and broken by proximity of the calibration slope to 1, and only then by parsimony. **No NHANES data were used in any selection or tuning step.** Complex-survey weights were handled by unweighted training with weighted evaluation (probed in sensitivity). LightGBM (0.840) and random forest (0.835) fell inside the 0.005 tie band, so the pre-specified tie-break applied and selected LightGBM, whose cross-validated calibration slope was 1.003 against 1.203 for random forest; elastic net (0.822) lay outside the band, and the parsimony criterion was never needed. LightGBM was refitted on the full 500,000-record training set; the selected hyperparameters and the full search grids are reported in the model card, which is deposited with the code (see Data Availability). The selected row-subsampling fraction is inactive in the deployed configuration because the subsampling frequency was left at its default of zero, so the fitted model uses all rows at every boosting iteration.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:83:### 2.12 Sensitivity analyses
docs\manuscript_17var.md:87:An adversarial audit corrected three silent data-processing defects before final analysis: (1) living-alone fabricated to a constant in NHANES pre (DMDHHSIZ absent ??constant imputation) ??corrected to preserve missingness; (2) NHANES post physical-activity coded 0 for all respondents due to string-typed unit fields mis-parsed to NaN ??corrected with explicit unit mapping; (3) an income refusal code (77777) not treated as missing, placing non-respondents in the top income quintile ??corrected. These affected data integrity but not the substantive conclusions.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:135:### 3.7 Sensitivity analyses
docs\manuscript_17var.md:143:On decision-curve analysis (weighted, with cohort-specific screen-all and screen-none default strategies), the model provided positive net benefit exceeding both defaults across a clinically relevant threshold-probability range in all three datasets (Figure 7). At the Youden-optimal threshold, computed over all observed scores rather than a coarse grid, sensitivity/specificity were 0.730/0.780 (internal), 0.733/0.687 (NHANES pre), and 0.739/0.667 (NHANES post); positive predictive value was low (0.110??.236) and negative predictive value high (0.948??.987), as expected for population screening at low prevalence (full operating characteristics across thresholds in Multimedia Appendix 3, Table S1). Tuned to a triage-appropriate 85% sensitivity, the model flagged roughly half of the external population (pre 51%, post 55%) at a PPV of 0.14 (pre) and 0.19 (post)?”a number needed to screen of 7.1 and 5.4?”and the 7-item deployment model performed comparably (PPV 0.137 and 0.184; number needed to screen 7.3 and 5.4). After local intercept/slope recalibration, net benefit remained positive and exceeded both default strategies?”screening everyone and screening no one?”at threshold probabilities of 0.10??.15 in every dataset; screen-all net benefit was in fact negative at these thresholds everywhere except NHANES post at 0.10, where the model still added +0.033 (Multimedia Appendix 3, Tables S4?“S5). Clinical utility remains provisional pending prospective evaluation.
docs\manuscript_17var.md:159:Cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset); differing measurement of alcohol, physical activity, income, BMI, and food insecurity; DPQ non-response of 14.3% (pre) and 32.7% (post) handled as complete-case and differential by education and race/ethnicity (ยง3.1), which may bias the external estimates?”especially the post-pandemic subgroup results?”in an unknown direction; living-alone unavailable in NHANES pre; food insecurity single-item and unmeasured in KCHS 2020??021; reduced discrimination in some racial/ethnic subgroups (wide CIs); low PPV at population prevalence (triage aid, not diagnostic); and prior depression diagnosis not recorded in KCHS 2017??025 (precluding a diagnosis-exclusion sensitivity analysis). Model development used a 500,000-record training set and a 100,000-record hold-out drawn from the 2,048,510 pooled records rather than the full pooled sample (ยง2.2); a training-set size experiment showed this did not cost external discrimination (ยง3.5), but internal estimates are hold-out rather than fully cross-validated. The 14-predictor comparator was trained with the same algorithm and a separately tuned configuration (ยง3.4), yet both models share a single preprocessing and evaluation pipeline, so the 14??7 comparison isolates predictor content rather than modeling strategy. The internal hold-out was drawn by simple random split, so 92.0% of its records belong to primary sampling units also represented in the training set; the internal estimate therefore describes new individuals from already-sampled clusters rather than wholly new clusters, and is expected to be mildly optimistic relative to a cluster-level split. The predictor-set amendment, although locked before any 17-predictor model was fitted, was written after the 14-predictor external result was known (ยง2.5.1). H2 and H4 were stated qualitatively in the protocol, without numeric decision margins, so judging them 'supported' involves interpretation. Finally, item reduction should be re-evaluated whenever the deployment population differs materially from the development population: adding smoking as an eighth item helps the pre-pandemic cohort but hurts the post-pandemic one (ยง3.6).
