.\docs\manuscript_17var.md:75:Discrimination (AUROC, AUPRC), calibration, and operating characteristics, all complex-surveyâ€“weighted. Calibration was summarized by the calibration slope and by calibration-in-the-large (CITL), the latter estimated as the intercept of a weighted logistic model with the linear predictor entered as an offset (ie, with the slope fixed at 1), together with the Brier score and expected calibration error (ECE); a positive CITL indicates that observed risk exceeds predicted risk. Ninety-five percent CIs were obtained by a rescaled stratified cluster bootstrap: within each design stratum, *n*<sub>h</sub>âˆ’1 primary sampling units were resampled with replacement and the survey weights rescaled by *n*<sub>h</sub>/(*n*<sub>h</sub>âˆ’1) times the selection count (1000 replicates) [16]. Rescaling is necessary because most NHANES strata contain only two primary sampling units, for which an ordinary within-stratum cluster bootstrap understates variance. We verified the implementation against a closed-form design-based (linearized ultimate-cluster) standard error for weighted depression prevalence: bootstrap 0.00466 vs design 0.00471 in the pre-pandemic cycle and 0.00802 vs 0.00796 in the post-pandemic cycle, i.e. agreement within 1%. Clinical utility by decision-curve analysis [8].
