29: ## Training data
30: - KCHS 2017?2025, community-dwelling adults ￣20 y with complete PHQ-9; pooled n=2,048,510; weighted depression prevalence 3.6%. Complex-sample (stratified, clustered, weighted). The model was fitted on an outcome-stratified random 500,000-record training set, with a separate 100,000-record hold-out used for internal metrics.
31: 
36: - Public, de-identified secondary data; no re-identification. Low PPV means many screen-positives are not depressed ? must be paired with confirmatory assessment and support pathways, not automated action. Subgroup performance differences (age, race/ethnicity) require monitoring to avoid inequitable triage.
37: 
38: ## Caveats and recommendations
39: - **Deploy only with local recalibration**: discrimination transports, but calibration drifts across country and pandemic; intercept/slope recalibration on local data restores it.
40: - **Cross-national ceiling ~0.77**: external discrimination is bounded by concept shift (predictor?outcome relationships differ between Korea and the US); this is intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, confirmed by an in-domain oracle (~0.77) versus within-Korea performance (~0.83) and by >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments.
41: - **Food insecurity** is the most weakly harmonized predictor (single item vs 10-item module; 4?5▼ prevalence difference); results are robust to its removal (16-predictor model).
42: - Self-reported inputs; cross-sectional (screens current symptoms, not future onset).
43: - Reproducibility: `17var_model/20_final_pipeline.py` (development, internal/external validation, parsimonious, sensitivity); `21_figures_tables.py` (SHAP, fairness, figures); `22_supplement.py`, `23_extra_metrics.py` (supplement); `41_audit_recompute.py`, `42_audit_tuning.py`, `43_consolidate.py` (verification re-runs). Results in `17var_model/results/final_*.csv`.
44: 
45: 
8: 
9: **What the study does.** We develop a machine-learning depression screener (PHQ-9 ￣10) in the Korea Community Health Survey (n?2.05 million) and externally validate it?without refitting?in the US NHANES across NCHS-defined pre- and post-COVID-19 cycles. This is, to our knowledge, among the first depression-screening models to be validated across country, health system, and the pandemic distribution shift using an identically defined outcome in both surveys.
10: 
11: **Why it matters for JMIR's readership.** Most published survey-based depression models report internal validation only and, in Korean data, achieve internal AUROCs up to ~0.90 while relying on predictors (e.g., perceived stress, depressed-mood items) that are near-tautological with the outcome. Our study instead asks the question that matters for any deployable digital screener: does it transport? We find that discrimination remains clinically useful but lower on external data (AUROC ~0.77), that calibration drifts but is fully recoverable by lightweight intercept/slope recalibration, and?critically?that this ~0.77 external ceiling is a *concept-shift* limit intrinsic to the transportable predictor set, not a modeling failure. We establish this rigorously with an in-domain oracle (fitting NHANES on its own labels caps at ~0.77, whereas the same predictors reach ~0.83 within Korea) and >450 leakage-controlled optimization experiments, and we show that predictor-importance rank order is highly concordant across surveys (Spearman 伏?0.97?0.99). A parsimonious 8-item version retains near-full external discrimination, supporting a low-burden web/mobile triage tool?squarely within JMIR's scope of internet-based health services.
12: 
13: **Methodological rigor and transparency.** The work follows TRIPOD+AI (2024; checklist enclosed as Multimedia Appendix 1) with PROBAST risk-of-bias self-assessment. We report a predictor-set amendment (14⊥17 predictors) that was documented and locked before analysis, with the added predictors chosen on survey availability and non-circularity rather than on any outcome association; all model fitting, tuning, algorithm selection, and the choice of deployment item count used development-data cross-validation only, and the 14-predictor comparator was given its own hyperparameter search so the comparison is not confounded by tuning effort. We also disclose and correct three silent data-processing defects found in an adversarial data-integrity audit. The analysis code, variable-harmonization dictionary, model card, and protocol/pre-specification documents will be deposited in a public repository on acceptance; generative AI tools assisted with code and drafting under full author verification.
14: 
15: **Declarations.** This manuscript is original, not under consideration elsewhere, and all authors approve submission. We declare no competing interests [to confirm]. The study uses public, de-identified secondary data; raw KCHS microdata are access-controlled and not redistributable, while NHANES data are openly available.

